![Completed vector route](images/a6/a6a7400a3b0aafed09994ce692174418fae00d0e56aade003e7ac77d7c922ca1.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{a}$.

Along $OX$, move $8$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares up. Thus $OX=4|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=4\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $2$ squares left and $2$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square left and $1$ square up. Thus $XC=2|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=2\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=4\mathbf{b}+2\mathbf{a}\\&=2\mathbf{a}+4\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=2$ and $\mu=4$.

